# Line endings and file types, stated rather than left to each machine.
#
# This repository is developed on Windows with core.autocrlf=true and built on
# Linux runners. That combination is fine for source files and fatal for two
# specific things, both of which arrived with the Gradle wrapper:
#
#   * gradlew is a SHELL SCRIPT that CI executes on Ubuntu. Committed with CRLF
#     it fails with "bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M" — a build failure whose
#     message says nothing about line endings.
#   * gradle-wrapper.jar is a BINARY. Any end-of-line translation corrupts it.
#
# Everything else is left to git's own detection, which is good at it.

# The wrapper script must reach Linux exactly as written.
gradlew text eol=lf

# ...and its Windows counterpart must keep CRLF, for the same reason in reverse.
gradlew.bat text eol=crlf

# Never translate anything inside an archive.
*.jar binary
*.zip binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.pdf binary
*.xlsx binary

# Shell scripts generally: db/tests/negative_checks.sh runs on the CI runner.
*.sh text eol=lf

# SQL migrations are applied by psql on Linux in CI and on Windows locally.
# LF everywhere keeps the checksum of a file the same on both.
*.sql text eol=lf
